---

<LessonLayout
  title="L19 — Debugging State Transitions"
>
  <LessonHeader
    num="L19"
    title="Debugging State Transitions"
    subtitle="Observe state at every node boundary, assert invariants to catch bugs early, and use stream mode to trace graph execution live."
  />

  <div class="lesson-content">

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">§3.3 — Why graph bugs are hard</h2>
    <p>
      A LangGraph execution is a sequence of state mutations across many nodes. When
      something goes wrong, the symptom (wrong final answer) is often far from the cause
      (a reducer bug in node 2 that corrupted state before node 5 even ran). Without
      observability at each transition, debugging is guesswork.
    </p>

    <LessonCallout variant="key" title="The debugging principle">
      <p>
        Bugs in graph state are like memory corruption bugs — the failure shows up far
        from the cause. Observe state at every boundary, not just the final output.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Technique 1 — Higher-order wrapper (withDebug)</h2>
    <p>
      Wrap node functions with a higher-order function (HOF) that logs state before and
      after. Strip the wrapper in production by checking <code>NODE_ENV</code>. This
      pattern is sometimes called "debug middleware" in functional programming jargon —
      it has nothing to do with NestJS HTTP middleware.
    </p>

    <CodeBlock lang="typescript" label="debug-middleware.ts" code={debugCode} />

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Technique 2 — Stream mode</h2>
    <p>
      LangGraph's <code>.stream()</code> accepts a <code>streamMode</code> option that
      controls the granularity of emitted events. <code>'updates'</code> mode shows exactly
      what each node wrote to state.
    </p>

    <CodeBlock lang="typescript" label="stream-debug.ts" code={streamDebugCode} />

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Technique 3 — Invariant assertions</h2>
    <p>
      State invariants are conditions that must always be true. Encoding them as runtime
      checks catches subtle reducer bugs — like an iteration counter not incrementing —
      before they propagate to later nodes.
    </p>

    <LessonCallout variant="tip" title="Common invariants to check">
      <ul>
        <li>Iteration counter is within the expected range (no runaway loops)</li>
        <li>Message history length is bounded (no context window overflow)</li>
        <li>Required state fields are present before the node that needs them runs</li>
        <li>Status values are from the allowed enum (no typos in router keys)</li>
      </ul>
    </LessonCallout>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Technique 4 — interrupt() as a breakpoint</h2>
    <p>
      Stream mode shows you what each node wrote — but the output is ephemeral.
      <code>interrupt()</code> gives you a persistent checkpoint at an exact node boundary
      so you can inspect the full state, patch values, and replay forward. Think of it as
      a debugger breakpoint, not a user-facing pause.
    </p>

    <LessonCallout variant="warn" title="Requires a checkpointer">
      <p>
        <code>interrupt()</code> only works when the graph is compiled with a checkpointer
        (<code>MemorySaver</code> for development, Postgres/Redis in production). Without
        one, calling <code>interrupt()</code> throws at runtime.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <p>The flow is three phases:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Phase 1</strong> — invoke the graph; it runs until the interrupt fires and returns a paused result containing <code>__interrupt__</code></li>
      <li><strong>Phase 2</strong> — call <code>graph.getState(config)</code> to inspect the full checkpoint; optionally call <code>graph.updateState(config, patch)</code> to correct state before resuming</li>
      <li><strong>Phase 3</strong> — resume by invoking again with <code>null</code> input and the same <code>config</code>; execution continues from the checkpoint</li>
    </ul>

    <CodeBlock lang="typescript" label="interrupt-debug.ts" code={interruptDebugCode} />

    <LessonCallout variant="tip" title="What this gives you that stream mode does not">
      <p>
        Stream mode logs are fire-and-forget. An interrupt saves a full checkpoint you
        can query with <code>getState()</code>, patch with <code>updateState()</code>, and
        replay any number of times. It is the difference between <code>console.log</code>
        and a real debugger — and it is especially useful when the bug only appears after
        several nodes have run.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Technique 5 — NestJS-idiomatic debugging</h2>
    <p>
      In a plain Node.js script you wrap node functions directly. In NestJS you have four
      interception mechanisms — each targeting a different layer. Choose by proximity to
      the bug.
    </p>

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>Scope</th><th>Use for graph debugging?</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>NestJS <code>NestMiddleware</code></strong> (HTTP)</td>
            <td>HTTP request / response, before routing</td>
            <td>No — HTTP-only; graph nodes are never reached from here</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Pipe</strong></td>
            <td>DTO validation / input transformation</td>
            <td>No — transforms DTO, doesn't trace node execution</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Interceptor</strong> (<code>NestInterceptor</code>)</td>
            <td>Controller method — request in, response out</td>
            <td>Yes — for HTTP-level logging at the agent endpoint boundary</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Method decorator</strong></td>
            <td>Individual class method on a service</td>
            <td>Best — wraps each node method with zero call-site changes</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <LessonCallout variant="key" title="Rule of thumb">
      <p>
        <strong>Standalone node functions</strong> — use <code>withDebug</code> from
        Technique 1 directly as a utility import. It is a pure function with no
        dependencies; wrapping it in an <code>@Injectable()</code> class adds DI
        ceremony with zero benefit.
        <strong>Class method nodes</strong> — use the <code>@TraceNode()</code>
        decorator. <strong>HTTP boundary</strong> — use a <code>NestInterceptor</code>.
        Avoid NestJS <code>NestMiddleware</code> — it's HTTP-only and the graph is
        never reached from that layer.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Pattern 1 — @TraceNode() method decorator</h3>
    <p>
      When your node functions live as methods on a NestJS service class, annotate them
      with <code>@TraceNode()</code>. The decorator intercepts each invocation
      transparently — no changes at call sites. Enable with
      <code>NODE_ENV=development</code>.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock lang="typescript" label="trace-node.decorator.ts" code={traceDecoratorCode} />

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Pattern 2 — NestJS Interceptor for HTTP-level tracing</h3>
    <p>
      When you need to observe what entered the agent endpoint and what it returned,
      attach an interceptor to the controller. This does not trace individual nodes —
      it records the full HTTP input/output pair with latency at the service boundary.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock lang="typescript" label="agent-trace.interceptor.ts" code={interceptorCode} />

    <LessonCallout variant="info" title="Production: use LangSmith">
      <p>
        All three patterns above are development tools. In production, enable
        <strong>LangSmith</strong> by setting <code>LANGCHAIN_TRACING_V2=true</code> and
        <code>LANGCHAIN_API_KEY</code> in your environment. LangSmith records every
        node's input, output, latency, and token count automatically — no code changes
        needed.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Common state transition bugs</h2>
    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Bug</th><th>Symptom</th><th>Root cause</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Wrong reducer</td>
            <td>Messages disappear after second node</td>
            <td>Replace reducer on a field that should accumulate</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Missing update</td>
            <td>Iteration counter stays at 0</td>
            <td>Node forgets to return the updated field</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Stale read</td>
            <td>Node uses old value despite earlier update</td>
            <td>Reading a field before its reducer has merged</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Router key typo</td>
            <td>Graph throws "no target node" at runtime</td>
            <td>Router returns <code>"Billing"</code> but map has <code>"billing"</code></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Exercises</h2>
    <p>Three problems — attempt each before revealing the answer.</p>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Exercise 1 — Spot the reducer bug</h3>
    <p>
      A chatbot runs for 3 turns. The stream output looks correct at each step but the
      final <code>messages</code> array contains only the last reply. Study the state
      definition below, identify the bug, and explain how to fix it.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock lang="typescript" label="buggy-state.ts" code={ex1BugCode} />
    <details class="dd-answer">
      <summary>Reveal answer →</summary>
      <div class="dd-answer-body">
        <p>
          The <code>messages</code> field uses a <strong>replace reducer</strong>
          <code>(_, n) =&gt; n</code>. Each time a node returns
          <code>&#123; messages: [reply] &#125;</code>, the reducer discards the
          existing array and replaces it with just the new one — so only the last reply
          survives. The <code>turnCount</code> reducer is identical but correct for a
          counter, because counters are replaced not accumulated.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock lang="typescript" label="fixed-state.ts" code={ex1FixCode} />
      </div>
    </details>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Exercise 2 — Write the @TraceNode() decorator</h3>
    <p>Implement a <code>@TraceNode(label?)</code> method decorator that:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Accepts an optional name string; falls back to the method name</li>
      <li>Logs <code>[ENTER label]</code> with the full state before the node runs</li>
      <li>Logs <code>[EXIT label]</code> with the update after the node returns</li>
      <li>Is a complete no-op when <code>NODE_ENV !== 'development'</code></li>
    </ul>
    <details class="dd-answer">
      <summary>Reveal answer →</summary>
      <div class="dd-answer-body">
        <CodeBlock lang="typescript" label="trace-node.decorator.ts" code={ex2AnswerCode} />
        <p>
          Key points: <code>descriptor.value</code> is the original method — replace
          it with a wrapper that calls <code>original.call(this, state)</code> to
          preserve the class context (<code>this</code>). Return the descriptor so
          TypeScript updates the prototype in place.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Exercise 4 — Add an interrupt breakpoint</h3>
    <p>
      You have a content moderation graph that routes by classification. The routing
      sometimes goes wrong but you cannot reproduce it reliably. Add an
      <code>interrupt()</code> inside the classification node so you can inspect the
      classification decision before routing fires. Then show how you would override the
      category to <code>'review'</code> before resuming.
    </p>
    <details class="dd-answer">
      <summary>Reveal answer →</summary>
      <div class="dd-answer-body">
        <p>Inside the node, call <code>interrupt()</code> after the LLM returns but before
        the <code>return</code> statement. Compile with <code>MemorySaver</code>, then use
        the three-phase flow: invoke → inspect → resume.</p>
        <CodeBlock lang="typescript" label="interrupt-debug.ts" code={interruptDebugCode} />
        <p>
          The key line for the override is
          <code>graph.updateState(config, &#123; category: 'review' &#125;)</code> between
          Phase 2 and Phase 3. On resume, the routing function will read
          <code>'review'</code> from the checkpoint, not the LLM's original answer.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Exercise 3 — Debug from stream output</h3>
    <p>
      You enable <code>streamMode: 'updates'</code> and immediately see a runtime crash.
      Read the stream snippet, identify the bug, and write the one-line fix.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock lang="typescript" label="router-bug.ts" code={ex3BugCode} />
    <details class="dd-answer">
      <summary>Reveal answer →</summary>
      <div class="dd-answer-body">
        <CodeBlock lang="typescript" label="router-fix.ts" code={ex3FixCode} />
        <p>
          Lesson: always normalise router output with <code>.toLowerCase().trim()</code>
          before it reaches an edge map. LLMs capitalise enum values inconsistently, and
          conditional edge routing is strictly case-sensitive.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <LessonChecklist
      title="What you can now do"
      items={[
        'Wrap node functions with <code>withDebug</code> middleware to log state transitions',
        'Write <code>withValidation</code> checks that assert state invariants before each node',
        'Use <code>graph.stream(input, &#123; streamMode: "updates" &#125;)</code> to trace execution live',
        'Diagnose wrong-reducer, missing-update, and router-key-typo bugs from state logs',
        'Use <code>interrupt()</code> as a debugger breakpoint to pause at any node, inspect the full checkpoint with <code>getState()</code>, patch state with <code>updateState()</code>, and resume with a null-input invoke',
        'Choose between <code>@TraceNode()</code> decorator, injectable service, or <code>NestInterceptor</code> based on where the bug lives',
        'Enable LangSmith with two env vars for zero-code-change production tracing',
      ]}
    />

