          hint: 'Streaming shows the skeleton — what changed. Think about what it cannot show.',
          answer: `<p>Streaming tells you <em>that</em> a node ran and <em>what</em> state changed. Custom logging tells you <em>why</em> — the business logic reasoning that never surfaces in state. Four scenarios where streaming alone is not enough:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Multi-step calculations inside one node.</strong> A pricing node that applies loyalty tier, promo code, and a cap produces a single state update <code>&#123; price: 42.50 &#125;</code>. Streaming shows the number. A custom log shows: <em>"Base: $50 → loyalty gold −10% → promo SUMMER20 −15% → cap applied → $42.50"</em>. Without the log, a wrong price is impossible to audit.</li>
  <li><strong>RAG retrieval quality.</strong> The retrieval node returns chunks. Streaming shows <code>&#123; chunks: [...] &#125;</code>. A custom log records cosine scores for every candidate: <em>"8 docs retrieved, scores [0.91, 0.87, 0.83, 0.71, 0.65, …], threshold 0.7 → 4 passed"</em>. You cannot diagnose poor answer quality without the scores — state only carries the winners.</li>
  <li><strong>Router confidence.</strong> A conditional edge fires and streaming shows the next node. A custom log adds: <em>"category=billing, confidence=0.51, threshold=0.7 → fell back to general (low confidence)"</em>. The routing decision is invisible to streaming because it happens in the edge function, not in a state field.</li>
  <li><strong>Prompt and model metadata.</strong> Streaming shows the LLM response. A custom log records the prompt template version, model, temperature, and token counts. This is the only way to understand regressions when a prompt is updated — state carries the output, not the inputs that produced it.</li>
</ul>
<p>Rule of thumb: use streaming as your first pass to find <em>which</em> node is suspicious. Add custom logging inside that node to find out <em>why</em> it produced the wrong output.</p>`,
